Chapter: RocMutableReassign
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: list alias mutable reassignment", case 12 in file order:

     { var $x = [1, 2] $x = [3, 4] match $x { [a, b] => a + b, _ => 0 } }

 and Roc expects 7.

 THE ONE ADAPTATION THAT IS NOT A STANDING ONE, and it is why cases 12 and 15
 sat deferred rather than being ported with the rest of the aliasing cluster.

 **Codex has no mutable LOCAL.** `mutable` is a property of a type
 declaration -- `mutable Box = record { n : Integer }` -- and mutation happens
 through a field of such a record. There is no `var` binding form; the
 language has no spelling for Roc's `var $x`.

 So the mutable location becomes a mutable record field. That is a real change
 of shape and not a rename: Roc rebinds a local, this stores into a heap cell.
 What survives is the whole of what the case asks -- a location holding a
 list, overwritten with a different list, then read -- and for a backend with
 real memory the record version is the harder of the two, because the old list
 must stay reachable long enough to be replaced and no longer.

 The remaining adaptations are standing: no list patterns, so `list-at`, and
 Roc's `7.0` is Codex's `7`.

Section: Types

  mutable Cell = record {
   lst : List Integer
  }

Section: Functions

  reassign : mutable Cell -> Integer
  reassign (c) =
    c.lst = [3, 4]
    list-at (c.lst) 0 + list-at (c.lst) 1

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (reassign (Cell { lst = [1, 2] })))
  end
